<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Levellers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1640s</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Levellers were a radical political movement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> era, led by John Lilburne, Richard Overton and William Walwyn. In pamphlets and the Agreement of the People they demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the people, a wide male franchise, religious toleration and equality before the law, debating them with army leaders at Putney in 1647. Cromwell crushed Leveller mutinies at Burford in 1649.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/levellers.jpg" alt="Levellers" width="172" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agreement_of_the_People_(1647-1649).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word leveller was used in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as a term of abuse for rural rebels; in the Midland Revolt of 1607 it named those who levelled hedges and walls in enclosure riots. As the name of a political movement it was first attached to a faction of New Model Army Agitators and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> supporters, said to be plotting to assassinate Charles I, and gradually to John Lilburne, Richard Overton and William Walwyn. Marchamont Needham, author of the newspaper Mercurius Pragmaticus, did much to fix it. The leaders denied wanting to bring everyone down to a common level, but adopted the name because most people knew them by it. The first recorded use is in a letter of 1 November 1647.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The campaigns to free Lilburne from prison, after his arrests in 1645 and 1646, gave rise to the movement. Overton wrote An Arrow Against All Tyrants and Tyranny while imprisoned in 1646. At the Putney Debates, held in St Mary's Church from 28 October to 11 November 1647, the Agitator Robert Everard presented the Agreement of the People, and Henry Ireton argued that only those with a permanent interest, meaning property, should vote. The Levellers' largest petition, presented on 11 September 1648, was signed by about a third of Londoners.</p><p>After the execution of Charles I in January 1649 the army's leaders banned soldiers' petitions. Robert Lockyer, a former Leveller agitator, was hanged on 27 April 1649, and a thousand men escorted his body wearing sea-green ribbons and rosemary. In May Cromwell attacked the Banbury mutineers, and three of their leaders were shot on 17 May, which destroyed the Levellers' base in the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Levellers published a newspaper, The Moderate, from July 1648 to September 1649. They would have extended the vote to almost all adult men but excluded wage-earners and those living on charity, and women. Unlike the Diggers they opposed common ownership. Their Agreement of the Free People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, written on 1 May 1649, includes the right to silence, since made law, and an elected judiciary, which has not been. By 1650 they were no longer a serious threat to those in power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Levellers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fifth Monarchy Men, a radical millenarian wing, agitated from the right wing of the movement, while sectarian groups such as the Ranters, <b>Levellers</b> and Quakers pulled from the left.</blockquote><a href="/movement/puritans">Puritans · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Levellers</b>, a minority movement mainly of Puritans, Presbyterians and Quakers, called for freedom of religion, frequent parliaments and equality under the law, and John Milton’s Areopagitica made one of the first arguments for freedom of speech.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 17th-century England libertarian ideas took modern form in the writings of the <b>Levellers</b> and John Locke, whose Two Treatises of Government (1689) held that people’s rights existed before government, that its purpose is to protect personal and property rights, and that people may dissolve governments that fail…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Some scholars have also retrospectively associated populism with the democracy of classical Athens, the <b>Levellers</b> of seventeenth-century England and the Protestant Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/populism">Populism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Levellers</b> and Diggers judged it a limited grant.</blockquote><a href="/work/magna-carta">Magna Carta · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The regime passed the Navigation Act of 1651, fought the First Anglo-Dutch War and allowed some religious toleration while repressing <b>Levellers</b> and royalists.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-england">Commonwealth of England</a></li></ul></section>
